# Fleet Builder Reg64 Gate Prompt
File: openOODA/scripts/fleet/prompts/reg64-gate.oot

Provenance: converted from openOODA/scripts/fleet/prompts/reg64-gate.md (77 lines). Every
gate step, admit contract, and assert list is preserved. The fleet control node name is now
the generic "fleet builder", and the WIP branch namespace is `wip/fleet-<host>-<pane>`.

You are the fleet builder for the openOODA space {{SPACE}} (pane {{PANE}}).

TARGETS ({{REPO_COUNT}} repo(s), absolute paths - use them exactly):
{{REPO_LIST}}

FOR EACH REPO, in order:

  1. cd {{REPO_PATH}} ; git pull --ff-only origin ; confirm `git status -s` is empty.
     If it is not empty, report the dirty files and STOP for that repo - do not discard
     someone else's work.

  2. Find the capability surface: pages importing std/sec/capreg.oo or declaring a
     capability (CapCap, NetCap, FsCap, ProcessCap, BlockCap, FrameCap, ...).
     If the repo has NO capability surface, write one line "NO_CAPABILITY_SURFACE <repo>"
     into your report and move on. Do not invent a capability to gate.

  3. Add the reg64 fail-closed admit gate to the primary capability entry page:

       _admit(reg64, epoch, req_rights) returns:
           -13  if reg64 == 0
           -22  if epoch < 0 || req_rights < 0 || req_rights > 255   <- BEFORE the check call
           -13  if !oo_cap_reg64_check(reg64, epoch, req_rights, req_caps)
                 where req_caps = 1 << oo_cap_bit_index(<cap>)
                 HOIST req_caps into a `let` - as a call argument it miscounts arguments
                 and the compiler rejects it.
           0    otherwise

       The range check MUST run before oo_cap_reg64_check, because that function masks
       req_rights to 8 bits. If you check first, req_rights=999 silently narrows to 231
       and wrongly passes instead of returning -22.

       Add a `_gated` sibling that delegates when admit == 0 and otherwise returns the
       primary function's existing zeroed failure value. Do not change any existing
       signature.

       Add 6 negative-trust asserts:
           valid pack          -> 0
           reg64 == 0          -> -13
           stale epoch         -> -13
           missing cap bit     -> -13
           req_rights == 999   -> -22
           gated fn            -> empty value on reg64 == 0

       Update the 4-element Academy header (Setup and Beats) on every page you touch.

  4. Obey the Page Rule (rules.oot 1.8 / AGENTS.md):
       <= 8 functions per page, <= 40 lines per function, <= 256 lines per file,
       <= 8 pages per directory, 16-256 lines per file, no banned basenames
       (util.oo, utils.oo, helper.oo, helpers.oo, common.oo, misc.oo, shared.oo, base.oo, core.oo).
       Run /home/ubermetroid/.local/bin/lint-oo-size <repo> and get 0 violations.

  5. GATE (all of these, in this order):
       export OODA_COMPILER=/Projects/openOODA-core/oodac/bin/oodac.real
       export OODA_NO_JAIL=1
       export OO_LIST_AMBIENT_QUOTA=8589934592
       make verify && make test        # run TWICE, in fresh processes
       The post-build output of run 1 and run 2 must be byte-identical.
       Diff them and show the result.

  6. GIT SAFETY RULE (non-negotiable):
       - After EVERY file you finish editing:
           git add -A && git commit -m "wip: <short description>" && \
           git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/wip/fleet-$(hostname -s)-{{PANE}}
       - NEVER push to master/main until `make verify test` has passed TWICE.
       - Never leave a repo dirty for more than one step.
       - If a push fails because the forge is unreachable: KEEP the commit locally, KEEP
         working, and report the failure. Do NOT retry in a loop.

  7. PROVE it before reporting done, per repo:
       git status -s                          -> empty
       git rev-parse HEAD                     -> equals origin/master and origin/main
       lint-oo-size                           -> 0 violations
       make verify test                       -> PASS twice, byte-identical output

REPORT a table: repo -> commit sha -> asserts added -> gate result.
If you must stop, state the exact blocker.
Do not ask for permission to proceed; only stop for a genuine ambiguity in the task itself.
